Day 261/300: Longest Balanced Subarray I

Problem Statement #3719

Problem Statement Explantion:

Given an integer array nums, find the length of the longest contiguous subarray where the number of distinct even numbers is equal to the number of distinct odd numbers.
A subarray is a contiguous non-empty sequence of elements within an array.

Solution Approach:

Since the constraints for this problem are relatively small (n≤1500), we can use a Brute Force approach with an optimized check for distinct elements.

Steps:
Nested Loops: We iterate through every possible starting position i and ending position j to examine every subarray nums[i...j].
Distinct Tracking: For each starting position, we reset a HashSet to keep track of unique numbers encountered as we expand the subarray.

Parity Counting:

If an element has not been seen in the current subarray, we check if it is even or odd.
We increment the respective distinctEven or distinctOdd counter.
Update Maximum: Whenever distinctEven == distinctOdd, we calculate the current subarray length (j - i + 1) and update our global maximum.

Here's the code:

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public int longestBalanced(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int maxLen = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();
            int distinctEven = 0;
            int distinctOdd = 0;

            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                if (!seen.contains(nums[j])) {
                    seen.add(nums[j]);
                    
                    if (nums[j] % 2 == 0) {
                        distinctEven++;
                    } else {
                        distinctOdd++;
                    }
                }

                if (distinctEven == distinctOdd) {
                    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, j - i + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        return maxLen;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(n^2)
Space Complexity: O(n)



